WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.700
Part two.

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.117
Five questions to ask any dataset.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:09.007
In October 2020, England's public health agency found

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:14.987
that nearly 16,000 positive COVID tests had been left out of its daily counts.

00:00:14.987 --> 00:00:16.837
There was no hacker.

00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:22.777
News reports traced the problem to an old spreadsheet format that ran out of rows.

00:00:22.777 --> 00:00:25.323
Contact tracing for those cases was delayed.

00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:31.035
Most data problems are this ordinary, and the fix is a habit.

00:00:31.035 --> 00:00:36.585
When a patient arrives, a doctor takes a history before deciding anything.

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:39.255
You can do the same with a dataset.

00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:40.895
Ask it five questions.

00:00:41.459 --> 00:00:44.149
Question one: where?

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:46.579
What place does this number describe?

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:52.119
A county, a ZIP code, the patient's home, or the clinic's address?

00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:56.149
Someone may live in one county and get care in another.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:58.579
Which county gets counted?

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:02.909
If you're deciding where a clinic should go, that answer changes everything.

00:01:03.459 --> 00:01:06.109
Question two: when?

00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:08.559
How many people live in Greene County?

00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:10.969
Here are four official answers.

00:01:10.969 --> 00:01:14.989
7,730 in the 2020 census.

00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:20.219
7,706 from a survey that combines five years of answers.

00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:24.239
7,317 estimated for 2023.

00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:28.179
And 7,067 estimated for 2025.

00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:29.149
All correct.

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:31.043
Each one answers a different when.

00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:34.784
Question three: what, exactly?

00:01:34.784 --> 00:01:38.234
What share of adults in Greene County have diabetes?

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:41.294
One CDC table says 27 percent.

00:01:41.294 --> 00:01:44.314
The same table also says 21.

00:01:44.314 --> 00:01:47.624
CDC's previous release says 19.

00:01:47.624 --> 00:01:50.244
Twenty-seven is the plain share of adults.

00:01:50.244 --> 00:01:54.544
Twenty-one adjusts for age, so an older county can be compared fairly

00:01:54.544 --> 00:01:56.054
with a younger one.

00:01:56.054 --> 00:01:59.634
Nineteen is the age-adjusted figure from the year before.

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.304
One disease, three honest numbers.

00:02:02.304 --> 00:02:03.895
Always read the definition.

00:02:04.445 --> 00:02:07.405
Question four: how sure?

00:02:07.405 --> 00:02:12.545
Almost every number here is an estimate, and estimates have ranges.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:19.975
In Jefferson County, 6.9 percent of households have no car, give or take 0.4.

00:02:19.975 --> 00:02:25.865
In Greene County, it's 13.2 percent, give or take 4.9.

00:02:25.865 --> 00:02:30.065
The true figure could be about 8, or about 18.

00:02:30.065 --> 00:02:33.388
Report the range, and you're being honest about what you know.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:37.045
Question five: who's missing?

00:02:37.045 --> 00:02:39.335
A blank doesn't mean zero.

00:02:39.335 --> 00:02:42.445
No air monitor doesn't mean clean air.

00:02:42.445 --> 00:02:45.385
No clinic visit doesn't mean no illness.

00:02:45.385 --> 00:02:47.065
People without a car, insurance,

00:02:47.065 --> 00:02:51.065
or internet are often the hardest to find in records,

00:02:51.065 --> 00:02:54.045
and the people who live there often know what the records miss.

00:02:54.045 --> 00:02:55.995
So ask them.

00:02:55.995 --> 00:03:00.665
And in a place this small, a small number can point to a real neighbor.

00:03:00.665 --> 00:03:01.728
Handle it with care.

00:03:02.278 --> 00:03:03.468
Where.

00:03:03.468 --> 00:03:04.748
When.

00:03:04.748 --> 00:03:06.258
What.

00:03:06.258 --> 00:03:07.668
How sure.

00:03:07.668 --> 00:03:09.328
Who's missing.

00:03:09.328 --> 00:03:13.788
Write your answers on a one-page Source Card, one for each dataset.

00:03:13.788 --> 00:03:17.418
It takes a few minutes, and your teammates will thank you.

00:03:17.418 --> 00:03:20.578
That's data curation, and it needs no code.

00:03:20.578 --> 00:03:22.041
Next, the tools.

